WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.704
Ohm's law questions often go wrong because the learner notices a changed number without first deciding what stayed fixed.

2
00:00:05.704 --> 00:00:11.407
But the whole lesson depends on that check.

3
00:00:11.407 --> 00:00:17.111
For an ohmic route, a larger voltage gives a larger current if the resistance stays fixed.

4
00:00:17.111 --> 00:00:22.815
A larger resistance gives a smaller current if the voltage stays fixed.

5
00:00:22.815 --> 00:00:28.518
The lesson stays rich when the learner sees a three-quantity relationship instead of a single slogan.

6
00:00:28.518 --> 00:00:34.222
That is what makes the worked example stable instead of lucky.

7
00:00:34.222 --> 00:00:39.925
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 5 becomes safer once current is read as the result of both voltage and resistance together.

8
00:00:39.925 --> 00:00:45.629
Fixed resistance means voltage drives the change.

9
00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:51.333
Raise the voltage on the same ohmic route and the current rises with it.

10
00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:57.036
Fixed voltage means resistance limits the rate.

11
00:00:57.036 --> 00:01:02.740
Make the route harder and the current falls even though the source stays the same.

12
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:10.318
This diagram matters because it keeps the relationship balanced.

13
00:01:10.318 --> 00:01:17.895
The source gives the per-charge push, the route supplies the opposition, and the resulting current is the rate that actually appears in the loop.

14
00:01:17.895 --> 00:01:25.472
If the route stays the same and the source push rises, more charge passes each second.

15
00:01:25.472 --> 00:01:33.050
If the source stays the same and the route gets harder, less charge passes each second.

16
00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:40.628
That is the visual story behind the formula.

17
00:01:40.628 --> 00:01:48.205
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong Ohm's law explanation keeps voltage, resistance, and current visible as different roles in one loop story.

18
00:01:48.205 --> 00:01:55.782
Push: Voltage is the source-side driver that can raise current when resistance is unchanged.

19
00:01:55.782 --> 00:02:03.360
Opposition: Resistance belongs to the route and can reduce current when voltage is unchanged.

20
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.151
A pressure-and-pipe analogy helps because it makes the two controls feel intuitive.

21
00:02:09.151 --> 00:02:14.942
If the push gets stronger while the route stays the same, the flow increases.

22
00:02:14.942 --> 00:02:20.733
If the route gets narrower while the push stays the same, the flow decreases.

23
00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:26.524
The analogy is not a full definition of electricity, but it is useful here because it teaches the learner to look for the driving side and the resisting side before naming the resulting rate.

24
00:02:26.524 --> 00:02:32.316
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy helps the learner expect current to rise with voltage and fall with resistance.

25
00:02:32.316 --> 00:02:38.107
More push means greater flow.

26
00:02:38.107 --> 00:02:43.898
At fixed route difficulty, a stronger drive increases the flow rate.

27
00:02:43.898 --> 00:02:49.689
More restriction means smaller flow.

28
00:02:49.689 --> 00:02:55.480
At fixed push, a tighter route decreases the flow rate.

29
00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:02.947
The core relation is current equals voltage divided by resistance.

30
00:03:02.947 --> 00:03:10.414
This means current is larger when the source push is larger and smaller when the route opposition is larger.

31
00:03:10.414 --> 00:03:17.881
The same relation can be rearranged into V equals I R or R equals V over I when a different quantity is unknown.

32
00:03:17.881 --> 00:03:25.348
But the learner should still hear the physical story underneath the symbols: push on top, opposition below, resulting rate in the answer.

33
00:03:25.348 --> 00:03:32.815
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene becomes strong once the learner can explain why changing voltage or resistance changes the current in opposite ways.

34
00:03:32.815 --> 00:03:40.282
Direct use: Use I = V / R when the current is unknown and the voltage and resistance are given.

35
00:03:40.282 --> 00:03:47.749
Rearrange carefully: Use R = V / I or V = I R when a different quantity is missing.

36
00:03:47.749 --> 00:03:55.216
fixed R: larger V gives larger I.

37
00:03:55.216 --> 00:04:02.683
Name the fixed quantity before substituting numbers.

38
00:04:02.683 --> 00:04:10.150
That is what makes the comparison trustworthy.

39
00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:16.361
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

40
00:04:16.361 --> 00:04:22.572
First lock the fixed quantity and say what stayed the same.

41
00:04:22.572 --> 00:04:28.783
Second choose the correct form of Ohm's law and substitute carefully.

42
00:04:28.783 --> 00:04:34.994
Third explain the result in words by linking the changed quantity to the changed current.

43
00:04:34.994 --> 00:04:41.205
This prevents the learner from treating the formula like a disconnected trick and makes follow-up comparison questions much easier.

44
00:04:41.205 --> 00:04:47.416
Keep this takeaway in view: Lock, substitute, and explain is the safest routine for every basic Ohm's law problem.

45
00:04:47.416 --> 00:04:53.627
Step 1 means lock the fixed quantity.

46
00:04:53.627 --> 00:04:59.838
Say whether voltage or resistance stayed constant before you calculate.

47
00:04:59.838 --> 00:05:06.049
Step 2 means use the correct form.

48
00:05:06.049 --> 00:05:12.260
Choose I = V / R, V = I R, or R = V / I based on the unknown.

49
00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:19.002
A learner can know the formula and still get the comparison wrong if the fixed quantity is not named first.

50
00:05:19.002 --> 00:05:25.744
Saying the resistor stays the same tells you the voltage change is the reason the current changed.

51
00:05:25.744 --> 00:05:32.487
Saying the source stays the same tells you the resistance change is the reason the current changed.

52
00:05:32.487 --> 00:05:39.229
This small verbal check gives the equation a direction and stops the learner from mixing two different cases together.

53
00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:45.971
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 5 gets stronger once the learner treats fixed-variable reading as part of the method, not as an optional comment.

54
00:05:45.971 --> 00:05:52.713
Same resistor means voltage drives change.

55
00:05:52.713 --> 00:05:59.456
With resistance fixed, current follows the voltage comparison.

56
00:05:59.456 --> 00:06:06.198
Same source means resistance drives change.

57
00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:12.940
With voltage fixed, current follows the resistance comparison in the opposite direction.

58
00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:18.645
The lesson says ohmic for a reason.

59
00:06:18.645 --> 00:06:24.350
An ohmic component obeys the simple proportional rule that lets voltage and current scale neatly at fixed temperature.

60
00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:30.055
That is why doubling the voltage on the same ohmic resistor doubles the current.

61
00:06:30.055 --> 00:06:35.760
The learner does not need a deep materials lecture here, but it is important to know the rule is attached to this kind of component.

62
00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:41.465
That keeps the law accurate instead of overgeneralized.

63
00:06:41.465 --> 00:06:47.170
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 5 stays accurate when the learner remembers that the neat proportional story belongs to ohmic components.

64
00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:52.875
Ohmic route means simple proportional rule.

65
00:06:52.875 --> 00:06:58.580
Voltage and current stay in a clean ratio when the route behaves ohmically.

66
00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:04.285
Why it matters means not every device is that neat.

67
00:07:04.285 --> 00:07:09.990
The lesson's clean scaling story belongs to ohmic components.

68
00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:14.750
Run four checks.

69
00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:19.510
For an ohmic route, current equals voltage divided by resistance.

70
00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:24.270
If resistance stays fixed, a larger voltage gives a larger current.

71
00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:29.030
If voltage stays fixed, a larger resistance gives a smaller current.

72
00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:33.790
And the first spoken move should be naming what stayed fixed.

73
00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:38.550
Those four checks capture the whole lesson before the arithmetic begins.

74
00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:43.310
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can justify a current change without guessing.

75
00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:48.070
Core rule means I = V / R.

76
00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:52.830
Current depends on both the source push and the route opposition.

77
00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:57.590
Fixed R means larger V, larger I.

78
00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:02.350
Same resistor means the current follows the voltage change.

79
00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:09.049
Start by asking whether the resistance stayed the same or whether the voltage stayed the same.

80
00:08:09.049 --> 00:08:15.748
Then use I equals V over R, or a rearranged form if a different quantity is missing.

81
00:08:15.748 --> 00:08:22.447
After calculating, say what the answer means in the loop: more current because the push increased, or less current because the route opposition increased.

82
00:08:22.447 --> 00:08:29.146
That is the cleanest route into the worked example.

83
00:08:29.146 --> 00:08:35.845
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 5 becomes much safer once the learner reads Ohm's law as a controlled comparison instead of as a bare symbol rule.

84
00:08:35.845 --> 00:08:42.544
Do not skip the fixed-variable line: That one sentence tells the worked example which comparison rule to use.

85
00:08:42.544 --> 00:08:49.243
Do not skip the final meaning: State whether the current changed because the push changed or because the route changed.

86
00:08:49.243 --> 00:08:55.942
name the fixed quantity first.

87
00:08:55.942 --> 00:09:02.641
use I = V / R or the correct rearrangement.

88
00:09:02.641 --> 00:09:09.340
The strongest answers sound like controlled comparisons, not like random substitutions.
